course: geography
lesson: 20
title: Canals: Suez, Panama and the shortcuts
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A canal is a waterway dug by people, a shortcut where nature left none. Where the land is flat, ships just sail through; where the land rises, locks lift them up like steps: a lock is a chamber with gates that fills or empties with water so a ship floats up or down.
explain: The Suez Canal, opened in 1869, joins the Mediterranean to the Red Sea with no locks, because both seas are at nearly the same level; it cut a voyage from London to Mumbai by about 40 percent compared with sailing around Africa.
explain: The Panama Canal, opened in 1914, needs locks, because ships climb about 26 metres to Gatun Lake and back down; every ship uses about 200 million litres of fresh water, so a drought in 2023 forced the canal to let fewer ships through.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 trade between Europe and Asia must sail around the Cape of Good Hope, and that is true for the game's whole period, because Suez opened long after the game's period ends. But canals are not new: China's Grand Canal is over 2,000 years old in parts, and the Canal du Midi was dug under Louis XIV.
explain: Think of a garden hose bent round a flower bed: cut a straight path across instead and the water arrives much sooner. A canal does that for ships, and when one stuck ship, the Ever Given, blocked Suez in 2021, hundreds of ships waited for six days.
fact: A canal is a waterway dug by people, and locks, which are chambers with gates, let ships climb or descend between different water levels.
fact: The Suez Canal, opened in 1869, is about 193 km long and joins the Mediterranean to the Red Sea without any locks.
fact: Using the Suez Canal shortens a voyage from London to Mumbai, in India, which Britain then ruled, by about 40 percent compared with sailing around the Cape of Good Hope.
fact: The Suez Canal was dug largely by Egyptian workers, tens of thousands of them forced labourers, and many died from disease and hard conditions.
fact: Egypt took over the Suez Canal in 1956, leading to the Suez Crisis, and since a big share of world trade passes through it, it mattered when in March 2021 the giant ship Ever Given got stuck across it for six days, holding up hundreds of ships.
fact: The Panama Canal opened in 1914 and is about 82 km long, using locks to lift ships about 26 metres up to Gatun Lake and back down again.
fact: A French attempt to dig a canal in Panama at sea level, without locks, in the 1880s failed after about 22,000 workers died, mostly from malaria and yellow fever, and the Americans later succeeded by fighting the mosquitoes that spread those illnesses and by using locks.
fact: Each ship passing through the Panama Canal uses about 200 million litres of fresh water, and a drought in 2023 forced the canal to limit the number of ships.
fact: China's Grand Canal, with parts over 2,000 years old, is the longest canal in the world at about 1,800 km, joining Beijing to Hangzhou.
fact: The Canal du Midi in southern France, built between 1666 and 1681, links the Mediterranean to the River Garonne, which flows to the Atlantic, so boats could avoid sailing all the way round Spain and Portugal.
fact: Britain's Bridgewater Canal, opened in 1761, carried coal to Manchester (a horse on the bank could pull a boat holding far more coal than a cart on a road) and began a canal age that spread thousands of kilometres of canals across England.
fact: The Kiel Canal in Germany, opened in 1895, links the North Sea to the Baltic Sea and carries more ships each year than any other ship canal.
fact: The Erie Canal, opened in 1825, linked the Great Lakes to the Hudson River, helping New York become the leading port of the United States.
exercise: What is a lock on a canal? | A chamber with gates that lets ships move between different water levels | A heavy chain stretched across the water that stops ships from leaving without paying | A bridge over the canal that lifts up so tall ships can pass under it | A big iron key that the keeper turns to open the canal each morning
exercise: Which two seas does the Suez Canal join? | The Mediterranean and the Red Sea | The Black Sea and the Caspian Sea | The North Sea and the Baltic Sea | The Atlantic and the Pacific Ocean
exercise: Why does the Suez Canal need no locks? | Both seas it joins are at nearly the same level | The ships that use it are all too small for locks | The water flows downhill all the way from the Mediterranean | Locks were banned by the Egyptian ruler in 1869
exercise: About how much does Suez shorten a voyage from London to Mumbai? | About 40 percent | About 4 percent | About 90 percent | It makes it longer
exercise: Who did most of the digging of the Suez Canal? | Egyptian workers, many of them forced labourers | French engineers and soldiers sent out from Paris | Steam machines, with almost no people working at all | British soldiers who were brought over from India
exercise: What happened in March 2021 in the Suez Canal? | The giant ship Ever Given got stuck for six days | A sandstorm filled the canal in and it had to be dug again | A brand-new set of locks was built in the middle of it | The canal froze over in a rare and bitter cold snap
exercise: What happened in 1956 that led to the Suez Crisis? | Egypt took over the Suez Canal | The canal first opened | Panama bought the canal | The canal dried up
exercise: How do ships cross the land in the Panama Canal? | Locks lift them about 26 metres up to Gatun Lake and back down | They are lifted onto long railway trains and carried across the hills | They sail straight through a cut dug at sea level the whole way | They are pulled over the hills on wooden rollers by teams of oxen
exercise: Why did the French attempt to dig a Panama canal fail in the 1880s? | About 22,000 workers died, mostly from malaria and yellow fever | The French ran out of shovels and spades and could not buy any more | The Americans stopped them by starting a war with France over it | The ground turned out to be solid granite that no tool could cut
exercise: Why can a drought slow down the Panama Canal? | Each ship uses about 200 million litres of fresh water for the locks | Ships need rain falling on their decks to keep their engines cool | The level of the whole Pacific Ocean drops when there is a drought | Long droughts make the steel lock gates rust and stick until it rains
exercise: Which is the longest canal in the world? | China's Grand Canal | The Suez Canal | The Panama Canal | The Kiel Canal
exercise: Which canal links the Mediterranean to the River Garonne? | The Canal du Midi | The Kiel Canal | The Göta Canal | The Erie Canal
exercise: What did the Bridgewater Canal carry to Manchester? | Coal | Oil | Bananas | Cars
exercise: Which canal carries more ships each year than any other ship canal? | The Kiel Canal | The Panama Canal | The Suez Canal | The Grand Canal
exercise: Which canal linked the Great Lakes to the Hudson River? | The Erie Canal | The Panama Canal | The Kiel Canal | The Canal du Midi
exercise: Which two seas does the Kiel Canal link? | The North Sea and the Baltic Sea | The Black Sea and the Aegean Sea | The Red Sea and the Arabian Sea | The Irish Sea and the Atlantic
test: In Europa Universalis 5 you can never sail through Suez. Is that a mistake in the game? | No, the canal opened in 1869, long after the game's period ends | Yes, Suez has been open since Roman times without a break | Yes, the Ottomans opened it in 1517, right in the middle of the game | No, because a canal at Suez has never been opened at all
test: «one boat gets stuck and the whole world freaks out?? lol» Which is the best answer? | A big share of world trade passes through one narrow canal, so a block delays goods everywhere | Only Egypt was really affected, because the ship only carried goods for Egyptian shops | The ship was carrying nearly all the world's food for that year, so people went hungry | Nothing really happened; it was just a news story that people made a fuss about online
test: Mia says canals are «a modern thing, like from the 1900s». What would you tell her? | Parts of China's Grand Canal are over 2,000 years old | She is right, the first canal ever dug was at Panama | Canals were first dug in the 1800s, when steam engines came in | Only the Romans ever dug canals, and nobody since
test: «why's panama got locks and suez hasn't, that's dumb» Which is the best answer? | Panama crosses hills, so locks lift ships; Suez crosses flat land between seas at about the same level | Suez is much older, and old canals were built before anyone thought they would need locks | Locks were only invented after Suez opened, so it was too late to add them to that canal | Panama's ships are far heavier, and heavy ships can only cross water if they use locks
test: What would happen if the Panama Canal's lake ran low in a long drought? | Fewer ships could pass, because the locks need lots of fresh water | Ships would sail faster, because shallow water slows them down less | The canal would fill with sea water and actually work better than before | Nothing at all, because the locks are worked with no water whatsoever
test: Why were canals cheaper than roads for moving heavy coal? | A horse could pull a much heavier load on a floating boat than on a cart | Coal floats by itself, so it could simply be pushed along the water | Roads were all toll roads, and canals were always free to use for everyone | Boats need nobody to steer them, so no wages had to be paid to boatmen
test: «what did the yanks learn from the french failing in panama» Which is the best answer? | To fight the mosquitoes that spread disease, and to use locks | To dig with machines only and never use any human workers at all | To build the canal through Nicaragua instead of Panama | To give up on canals and build a railway instead
test: Which long voyage did the Canal du Midi help ships avoid? | Sailing all the way round Spain and Portugal through Gibraltar | Sailing all the way round Africa past the Cape of Good Hope | Sailing round South America through the Strait of Magellan | Sailing round Britain and Ireland through the stormy North Sea
test: Why did the Suez Canal make Egypt so important to Britain? | It was the shortcut on the sea route to India | Egypt sold Britain the cotton for all its mills, and only the canal could carry it | Britain needed the Nile for drinking water | The canal was the only way to reach Australia
test: «erie canal, so what, it's just a ditch» Which is the best answer? | It linked the Great Lakes to the Hudson River and helped New York become the leading US port | It was just a small ditch that nobody ever used, and it was filled in after a few years | It linked the Great Lakes to the Mississippi and made New Orleans the leading US port | It was a canal for carrying drinking water to New York City, not for boats
test: A ship climbs 26 metres in the Panama locks to Gatun Lake. What must happen before it reaches the Pacific? | It must be lowered about 26 metres again through more locks | It must climb another 26 metres, because the Pacific is higher still | It must be carried down to the ocean by a giant crane | It must wait for high tide to float it over the hills
exam: Identify the principal function of a lock upon a canal. | The raising or lowering of vessels between stretches of water situated at differing levels | The prevention of vessels from departing the canal prior to the payment of the requisite toll | The storage of cargo in secure chambers during the hours of darkness | The elevation of bridges for the passage of tall vessels
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Suez Canal? | It was opened in 1869, connects the Mediterranean Sea with the Red Sea, and contains no locks | It was opened in 1914, connects the Atlantic with the Pacific, and contains numerous locks | It was opened in 1825, connects the Great Lakes with the Hudson River, and contains many locks | It was opened in 1895, connects the North Sea with the Baltic Sea, and contains two locks
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which the French attempt of the 1880s to construct a canal across Panama was abandoned. | Approximately twenty-two thousand workers perished, principally from malaria and yellow fever | The project was prohibited by the Government of Spain, which then still ruled the isthmus of Panama | The requisite funds were diverted to the enlargement of the Suez Canal | The isthmus was found to contain insufficient water for the operation of any locks
exam: Given that each transit of the Panama Canal consumes approximately two hundred million litres of fresh water, determine the consequence of a severe drought such as that of 2023. | The number of vessels permitted to transit the canal is restricted | The canal is filled with sea water, whereupon transits increase | The duration of each transit is reduced by one half | No consequence whatsoever ensues
